WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
Hey everyone, I'm here at SAGE Summit 2015 in New Orleans

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.340
and I just came out of a great session with Stephen Kelly

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.180
and Colin Powell and Deepak Chopra

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.300
and they were talking about some great strategies

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.060
and ideas to help you grow your business

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.380
and I'm going to share with you some of the highlights

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.300
from that speech today.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:45.580
If you ask people globally if they love what they do every day in their jobs,

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.580
only 20% of people say yes.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:58.580
In the United States alone, we have $300 billion lost a year

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.580
from disengaged or actively disengaged people in their jobs.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.580
What's the difference between actively disengaged and disengaged?

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.580
The actively disengaged are not only unhappy,

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.580
but they come to work with the intention of making everyone else unhappy.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.580
So this is a huge burden in our jobs.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.580
If a supervisor or a manager ignores their employee,

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:33.580
a particular employee, the disengagement score goes up by 45%.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.580
If on the other hand, they don't ignore them,

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.580
but they criticize them, actually the employee gets better,

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:45.580
the disengagement rate falls to less than 20%

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.580
because people would rather be criticized than ignored.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.580
But if you notice a single strength that they have,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.580
the disengagement rate goes down to less than 1%.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:04.580
In the job world, you have to focus on three things.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:07.580
You have to have inspired teams,

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:14.580
and inspiration comes from you, the leader of the workforce.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:19.580
And that is you give them attention, which means you listen to them,

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:27.580
appreciation, affection, to some extent acceptance, even for their weaknesses.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.580
You make sure that you all have shared vision,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.580
and then you complement each other's strengths,

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.580
because not everybody has the same strengths.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.580
That's what a good sports team is,

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.580
so as people who give employment to others,

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:48.580
think like a sports coach, shared vision, complementing strengths,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.580
emotional connections.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.580
If you do that, your business will be very successful.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:03:00.580
In fact, of the three constituencies that go to make an effective business,

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:05.580
which is investors, employees, and customers,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.580
your employees are number one.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:12.580
And your customers will be happy if your employees are happy,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.580
and your investors will be happy if your customers are happy.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.580
You might be your own investor.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.580
You can actually track the success of a business

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.580
just by what is called employee turnover rate.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.580
If the employee turnover rate is very high, you better watch out.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:30.580
And then there are other things.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.580
Engaging with your community,

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.580
but now you could have a global community, even with a small business.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.580
Making sure you connect with other people who have strengths

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.580
that comfortable with yours,

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.580
and that also is available now through LinkedIn

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.580
and many other social networks.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.580
And taking care of yourself,

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.580
which means getting enough sleep every day,

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.580
managing stress through whatever means you do.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.580
For me, it's meditation.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.580
Making sure you get exercise.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.580
Remaining emotionally healthy with love, compassion, joy,

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:11.580
equanimity, and proper nutrition.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.580
If you pay a little attention to just these things,

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.580
your personal well-being, your social well-being,

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:23.580
your community well-being, career well-being, financial well-being,

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.580
you're off to a good start.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.580
One day when I was Secretary of State, I got bored.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.580
And I wanted to go downtown.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:37.580
And I wanted to meditate or go away somewhere else.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.580
But I decided to go down to the garage.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.580
Because the garage and state department are small,

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.580
considering all the cars that come in every morning.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.580
And I wanted to see how the guys did it.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:50.580
So I escaped my bodyguards and assistants to the back elevator and went to the garage.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.580
The garage would run out of contract employees.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.580
All in the rest.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.580
Make a few bucks over minimum wage.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.580
And so I'm wandering around and they stop me.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.580
And so they come over.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:01.580
Mr. Secretary, you lost me.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.580
I'll help you get back upstairs.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.580
I said, I'm fine, thank you.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.580
And they said, what are you doing down here?

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:08.580
I wanted to talk to you guys.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.580
I wanted to see how you got all the cars in the morning.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.580
I know there are so many that you have to stack them one, two, and three.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.580
And two and three can't leave until one leaves.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:21.580
So how do you decide every morning who's number one, who's number two, who's number three?

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:22.580
Is the job okay?

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:24.580
Too much carbon dioxide?

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:25.580
Number five, sir.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:26.580
Thank you so much.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.580
Well, tell me, how do you decide who's number one?

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:31.580
They said, Mr. Secretary, you kind of go like this.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:38.580
When you pose in the morning, if you stop by us, if you go over here one day, as you look at me,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.580
and if you smile, I need to say, good morning, how are you?

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.580
You're number one.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:47.580
But we don't know what you're number one.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.580
That's all we're here for.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:50.580
I'm number one.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.580
There's really plenty.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:06:00.580
In that car is an assistant secretary who is here in the organization, in charge of many countries.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:06.580
Outside that car is his different contract employee, Hugo Soren in the donation.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.580
They both have dreams, fears, anxieties, and ambitions.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.580
Both of them.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:19.580
The one in the car has the option of helping the one outside the car achieve his dreams and ambitions

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.580
and minimizing his fears.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.580
And so that's their structure in the organization.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.580
But at that moment of good morning, they're equal.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:33.580
They're two human beings recognizing each other as human beings

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:37.580
and pay and respect solely for the fact that we are united as human beings

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:39.580
and together we can accomplish great things.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.580
Thank you all.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:59.580
Deepak, how do you attract the best people and keep the best people?

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:10.580
I personally recommend seven rules that have expanded on the seven spiritual laws for businesses.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.580
The first is be a really good listener.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:22.580
And that means be a good observer of what's happening in the company, the employees and the customers.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:32.580
Listen to emotional cues because lots of times people will be unhappy but you can read their body language.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:42.580
Listen with your soul. Take time to reflect on what is the zeitgeist at the moment in the business world.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:48.580
One of the things that I noticed as part of the zeitgeist is contribution to community.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:54.580
Your own company does that. Two percent of your income goes to supporting people.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:58.580
And so look to how you can contribute to your community.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:02.580
Second, use emotional intelligence.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:16.580
Emotional intelligence means the ability to feel emotions, empathy and communicating in a way that doesn't make people feel insecure.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:30.580
Third, expand your awareness. Constantly ask yourself what are we observing, what are we feeling, what's the current need in the business and what's the remedy?

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.580
How do we fulfill that need?

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:41.580
You can ask that of your customers. What are you observing? What are you feeling? What do you need? How can I help you to fulfill that need?

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:52.580
Third, be action oriented. It's good to philosophize but you have to be a role model for action.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:09:00.580
Develop smart goals every year. Smart, I use as an acronym, stretch more than you can reach.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:06.580
Make everything measurable. Make sure that your team, your employees also agree.

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:12.580
Keep a record of your progress and set time limits.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:26.580
Empower yourself with self-esteem. I read in this book yesterday that you have to be immune to criticism even though you are responsive to feedback.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:33.580
Because criticism can let you down if you take it personally. Take responsibility for your health.

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:48.580
As a 12 year old sweeping the floor, packing coffee bags, I knew from that very early age the connection between work, life and family.

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:54.580
And the great story of personal sacrifice for business owners.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:10:01.580
So why do we care at Sage so much for businesses like yours?

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:09.580
Around the world, small and medium businesses are the lifeblood of the economy.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:26.580
Yes, in this USA alone, 66%, yes two thirds of the 12 million new jobs created since 2010 were created by small and medium businesses like yours.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:35.580
Many of you work more than 6 yards a week. You are the first ones in and you are the last ones out every day.

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:42.580
You give up your holidays, you mortgage your homes and you invest your life savings in your businesses.

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:53.580
All to provide for your families, to create a legacy, to pursue your passion, to live your dreams.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:11:01.580
You are the true heroes of the economy, the risk takers, the winners in America and every economy around the world.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:13.580
This is truly a noble cause for prosperity. And that is why, that is why we care so much.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:25.580
At Sage, we feel such a huge sense of responsibility as your trusted technology partner.

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:31.580
We are your champion. We will fight for you. We will fight for the little guys.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:38.580
We will deliver products for you that let you work the way that you want to work.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:49.580
And that is why we are here today, here in New Orleans at the largest small and medium business conference in the world.

